1. **HTML Structure**: Create an input field and an SVG icon next to it.
2. **CSS Styling**: Use CSS to position the SVG icon to the right of the input field.
3. **JavaScript**: Add an event listener to the SVG icon to call the desired function when clicked.

Here's an example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with Clickable Icon</title>
    <style>
        .input-container {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }
        .input-container input {
            padding-right: 30px; /* Space for the icon */
        }
        .input-container .icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 0;
            top: 0;
            bottom: 0;
            cursor: pointer;
            fill: #000; /* Change color as needed */
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" placeholder="Type something...">
        <svg class="icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27C15.41 12.59 16 11.11 16 9.5 16 5.91 13.09 3 10.5 3S3 5.91 3 9.5 5.91 16 9.5 16 13.09 14.5 14 15.5z"/>
            <path d="M10 16.5l.5-.5.5.5-.5.5-.5-.5z"/>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        document.querySelector('.icon').addEventListener('click', function() {
            const input = document.querySelector('input');
            const inputValue = input.value;
            // Call your function here
            console.log('Input value:', inputValue);
            // Example function
            function onClickFunction() {
                alert('Input value: ' + inputValue);
            }
            onClickFunction();
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

